sand miners dive under the waves again  and again filling their nets with heavy  shell sand what they're doing is illegal  but building contractors pay a high  price for the sand  the miners earn nearly 10 euros a day  because of the mining the sea is  steadily encroaching on the land the  beach beside Togo's capital low May is  gradually disappearing I fix Otto and  his friends pray every morning for the  sand mining to stop  they also pray for a good harvest just a  stone's throw from the beach he and his  family have a fruit and vegetable garden  his garden is planted in the sand and  produces enough to feed the family when  it's hot the garden needs to be watered  several times a day you can harvest the  vegetables after a few weeks we pull out  the weeds and put down fresh dung and a  month later we get more vegetables we  repeat this four to five times a season  everything depends on the condition of  the soil but the garden is that risk of  being washed away by the sea Kimiko CUDA  heads an initiative called permaculture  Togo everything he and his colleagues  have planted on the beach has grown in  the last year they want to show how part  of the beach can be turned into a fruit  and vegetable garden  permaculture means building up a  sustainable form of organic farming that  relies solely on renewable resources  that nature itself provides a single  coconut palm can't hold back the sea but  if you plant many palms together like  here then the roots soon interlink they  pull together if you like and then they  have more of a chance against the waves  but for that to work the ground has to  retain its moisture natural materials  like shrub  animal dung and wood shavings are added  to the sand which allow it to retain  water and keep the plants strong and  healthy in five years time the coconut  and banana trees will be large enough to  stop the sand from washing away and will  also feed more families the sand mining  and the bigger waves are a huge problem  a